The RNA-seq data from the previously mentioned study was used for analysis to test if gene expression can predict gender.
Two data frames were created to hold the raw count data as well as log-normalized. 
These data frames were fed to a logistic regression function where results were tabulated including standard errors and p-values and results data frames were generated.
The top 10 genes were reported and there were a few that upon first look predict gender including SUPT20HL1 and ENSG00000229491.
There may be a few that are biologically relevant for gender, but the p-value distribution is rather balanced from 0 to 1, indicating that overall the association may be weak.

For the second part, multiple hypothesis testing using leave-n-out re-sampling was performed for both raw and transformed data with 100 iterations.
The leave one out sampling technique seems to have a strong effect as the top genes with this technique do not match the top 10 genes from more basic logistic regression.
This may not be as trustworthy with the sample size and leave out sampling as there is a much greater difference between the p-values of this set and the previous logistic expression.
Additionally, the raw and normalized data had the same results which means that normalization had no impact.
